n64recomp: a place the runtime can take the thread away

Only one recompiled thread runs at a time and the runtime chooses which, but
it only gets to choose when the running thread calls into it. On the console
that choice is made by an interrupt, at any instruction at all: a thread that
waits by reading memory is preempted, the thread that writes the memory runs,
and the wait ends.

`spin_wait` already covers the smallest form of that -- a loop the recompiler
can prove reads memory and does nothing else. Banjo-Tooie has a bigger one.
Between the end of its opening cutscene and the title screen it walks its
sixty sound emitters, stops each one, and goes round again until none is
still playing. That loop calls functions, so the recompiler cannot prove
anything about it, and it never asks the runtime for anything -- so the
retrace never arrives, the audio thread never runs, the sound never finishes,
and the console stops dead with every other thread waiting on a message that
is sitting in a queue nobody will drain.

So: every function entry is a place the thread can be handed over. The check
is a load of a word and a branch that is never taken, and the word is only
set when there is something to hand over to. Any loop that calls anything is
now interruptible, which together with `spin_wait` is every loop that can
wait for another thread.

The status register goes with the call because a game that has turned
interrupts off has said, in the console's own terms, that this is not a
moment it may be interrupted at.

The emission side of this -- the YIELD_POINT the recompiler writes at the head
of every function -- is carried by 0001 and 0008, which were regenerated from a
tree that already had it. This patch is the declaration alone; the hunks that
were also in those two are gone, because applying a change twice is a patch
series that does not apply to a fresh clone.

--- a/include/recomp.h
+++ b/include/recomp.h
@@ -439,6 +439,29 @@
 void switch_error(const char* func, uint32_t vram, uint32_t jtbl);
 void do_break(uint32_t vram);
 
+// A place the runtime is allowed to take the thread away.
+//
+// Only one recompiled thread runs at a time and the runtime chooses which, but
+// it only gets to choose when the running thread calls into it. On the console
+// that choice is made by an interrupt, at any instruction at all: a thread that
+// waits by reading memory in a loop is preempted, the thread that writes the
+// memory runs, and the wait ends. Here it never is, so a game that waits that
+// way waits forever -- and games do, for a sound to finish or a lock to clear.
+//
+// `spin_wait` below covers the smallest form of it, a loop the recompiler can
+// prove reads memory and does nothing else. This covers the rest: every
+// function entry is a point where the thread can be handed over, so any loop
+// that calls anything is interruptible. The cost when nothing is waiting is a
+// load of a word and a branch that is never taken.
+//
+// The status register goes with the call because a game that has turned
+// interrupts off has said, in the console's own terms, that this is not a
+// moment it may be interrupted at -- and the whole point of this is to be the
+// interrupt.
+extern volatile unsigned recomp_preempt_wanted;
+void recomp_preempt(uint8_t *rdram, unsigned status_reg);
+#define YIELD_POINT() if (recomp_preempt_wanted) { recomp_preempt(rdram, ctx->status_reg); }
+
 // The function signature for all recompiler output functions.
 typedef void (recomp_func_t)(uint8_t* rdram, recomp_context* ctx);
 // The function signature for special functions that need a third argument.
